    "two_by_two_detector_x_tracker_k1": {
      "_design": "baseline stock YOLOv8n vs delivered E35, each with four trackers, k=1, board INT8",
      "baseline_det": {
        "ours": 0.5206,
        "bytetrack": 0.5185,
        "botsort": 0.5207,
        "ocsort": 0.5157
      },
      "e35_det": {
        "ours": 0.6126,
        "bytetrack": 0.6232,
        "botsort": 0.6261,
        "ocsort": 0.5951
      },
      "delta_e35_minus_baseline": {
        "ours": 0.092,
        "bytetrack": 0.1047,
        "botsort": 0.1054,
        "ocsort": 0.0793
      }
    },
    "findings": [
      "At k=1 all four implementations sit in a 0.556-0.626 band; ours 0.6126 is mid-band and BoT-SORT-default (0.6261) is the best. The shipped tracker is competitive but not superior.",
      "The model-side gain is tracker-independent: with ByteTrack/BoT-SORT/OC-SORT the same E35 detector gains +0.079..+0.105, equal to or larger than with our own tracker (+0.092).",
      "At k=4 the published defaults degrade sharply: ByteTrack 0.1621, BoT-SORT 0.1625, OC-SORT-default 0.0004 (four-session means), versus ours 0.5655 and OC-SORT with min_hits=1 0.5084.",
      "Emitted-track counts explain it: at k=4 ByteTrack/BoT-SORT emit 7-23 tracks (ours 187-474) and OC-SORT-default reports only on just-updated frames. Their confirmation policy assumes a detection on essentially every frame.",
      "Frame-level diagnosis (data/tracker_k4_diagnosis.json) localises the failure: the signature is tiny FP (11-1218) with huge FN - the tracker stops creating tracks (session 60: 11 tracks over the whole 1470-frame video, only 1-5 activated tracks mid-video, FN 15758 of ~19403 GT, FP 11 -> MOTA 0.187). Cause: the published code deletes unconfirmed (newborn) tracks on any call where they are not matched, and under k=4 a newborn's second observation arrives 4 frames later while its zero-velocity Kalman prediction barely moves, so the IoU gate necessarily fails.",
      "Scope of the fix matters: skipping the deletion only on detection-free frames is too narrow (0.1514/0.1520); patching the unconfirmed step itself (never declare a newborn unmatched, 5 lines) restores ByteTrack to 0.5662 and BoT-SORT to 0.5696 (four-session means) - on par with our 0.5655. So the boundary is a specific confirmation policy in the published implementations, not an inherent limitation of strided tracking."
    ],
